---
// The Eater Map Section, recomposed to the reference by #191: three standing
// blocks rather than a band of writing over a picture. The word PROJECTS at the
// head of the left column — the Gallery's own masthead, in the Gallery's own
// box — with the serif project title under it and the copy at that
// column's foot; the Exploded View down the middle, the captured Slab standing
// still under one parallel projection with the app's own three surfaces raised off
// it; and the four numbered points down the right edge, each joined to the part it
// names by a rule that ends in a lit dot.
//
// NOTES.md carries the reasoning: how the masthead hits the Gallery's box and why
// that means this Section HAS joined the landing measure, why the title's two
// ratios need a script, and the two pieces of arithmetic the stage turns on.
//
// THE MARKUP RESTS IN THE RAISED STATE, and that inverts the obvious build
// (#177). The Lift animates FROM flat TOWARDS raised, so the finished Exploded
// View is what a reader whose scripts never arrived and a reader who asked for
// reduced motion each get for nothing, and nothing here is contingent on a
// script. Flat is `--eater-map-lift: 0` and it is where the motion begins.
import { CARDS } from './cards';
import { content } from './content';
import { markFor } from './icons';
import { SLAB_PART } from './leaders';
import { SLAB } from './slab';
// The Eater app's own stylesheet, vendored with the Cards (#174). A plain CSS
// file, so Astro does not scope it — every selector in it begins with the
// `.eater-cards` host, and that host is the containment. NOTES.md.
import './assets/cards/cards.css';
// The fourth surface's own stylesheet, and it is a second file rather than an
// edit to the one above because it came out of a second run of the same
// collector — the three off `/export`, the dropdown off the running app, which
// is the only way to get it without changing the harness in that repository
// (#194). `design/eater-cards/README.md` is the authority.
import './assets/cards/results.css';
// The one thing this Section says to the app's own markup that the dark theme's
// inherited custom properties cannot carry: a corner. Plain CSS and not the
// scoped block below, because a `border-radius` needs a SELECTOR and a scoped
// selector cannot reach markup that arrived through `set:html`. After the export
// on purpose, though it does not depend on the order — the file says why.
import './cards-shape.css';
import './tokens.css';
// variants.css is deliberately NOT imported: an unselected Variant has to cost
// the shipped page nothing, so the sheet is not part of the build at all and the
// render tool injects it. NOTES.md, and check-source.mjs if this is ever undone.
---

{/* data-section is the loader's handle: it names the Section whose timeline.ts
    is fetched as this element approaches the viewport, and it is what makes this
    a resting place — src/kernel/landing.css declares every Section after the
    first a port, as a relationship rather than a list, so nothing in the Kernel
    had to learn this Section's name.

    No data-turn. The crossing spans the FIRST Section and is finished by the
    time the Projects Panel comes to rest, so the page is already dark when the
    reader arrives here and there is no second crossing to run. src/kernel/turn.ts. */}
<section
  class="eater-map"
  id="eater-map"
  data-section="eater-map"
  aria-labelledby="eater-map-masthead"
>
  {/* No Rail here. It is one piece of furniture standing on the page and the
      Kernel's (#192) — this Section drew a second copy of it and swapped it in
      as the reader turned, which is the thing that ticket deleted. What is left
      of it here is the column it stands in, which is the page's own left margin
      and is what the padding below leaves. */}
  <div class="eater-map__inner">
    {/* PROJECTS, IN THE GALLERY'S OWN BOX (#191). The word the reader met on the
        Panel, drawn again here at the landing's own size and standing at the
        landing's own margin — so turning onto this screen does not move it. It is
        still THIS Section's element and this Section's accessible name: making the
        page's PROJECTS one persistent thing is #193, and the slot it takes over is
        this one.

        THE SERIF TITLE UNDER IT IS SIZED AND PLACED BY `title.ts`, off the
        masthead's real ink, because the two ratios the reference states are
        between CAP HEIGHTS and a stylesheet can only divide font sizes. The
        stylesheet's fallbacks are a whole title about four per cent small rather
        than a hole, which is what a reader whose scripts never arrived gets. */}
    <header class="eater-map__head">
      <h2 class="eater-map__masthead" id="eater-map-masthead" data-eater-map-masthead>
        {content.masthead}
      </h2>
      <p class="eater-map__title" data-eater-map-title>
        {content.title.map((line) => (
          <span>{line}</span>
        ))}
      </p>
    </header>
    <div class="eater-map__copy">
      {content.copy.map((paragraph) => (
        <p>{paragraph}</p>
      ))}
    </div>
    {/* THE EXPLODED VIEW: one still of the map, and the app's own three surfaces
        drawn on it at the scale the map is drawn at, so that flat it is
        indistinguishable from a screenshot of the phone — and is made of a
        picture and real text rather than of pixels. Raised, the same picture and
        the same three surfaces come apart under one camera.

        The two numbers below are the ONLY thing the markup says about geometry,
        they are not chosen, and they are not repeated anywhere: they are the
        capture's own, out of slab.ts, and every other length on this plane is
        derived from them or is a Token. A re-capture at another phone size moves
        them and the composition follows. NOTES.md.

        FOUR BOXES AND EACH HAS ONE JOB, which is one more than the flat state
        needed and is forced rather than chosen — NOTES.md has the two rules that
        force it. The Slab is the SIZE and the container the plane's lengths are
        shares of; the plane is the CAMERA and the one rotation everything on it
        shares; the Cards' box is the app's own stylesheet host; and each Card is
        a depth off the plane's surface. */}
    <div class="eater-map__stage">
      <div
        class="eater-map__slab"
        data-eater-map-watch
        data-eater-map-slab
        style={`--eater-map-app-w: ${SLAB.viewport.width}px; --eater-map-slab-ratio: ${SLAB.pixels.width} / ${SLAB.pixels.height}; --eater-map-slab-tall: ${SLAB.pixels.height / SLAB.pixels.width}`}
      >
        <div class="eater-map__plane" data-eater-map-plane>
          {/* `lazy`, so the Slab's quarter of a megabyte arrives as the reader
              comes down the page rather than at load — the same promise the
              Section's own chunk makes, kept declaratively so it holds with no
              script at all. `alt` is Content: the picture carries a claim about
              the project and a reader who cannot see it is owed the claim. */}
          <img
            class="eater-map__still"
            data-eater-map-still
            src={SLAB.src}
            width={SLAB.pixels.width}
            height={SLAB.pixels.height}
            alt={content.stage.slabAlt}
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
          {/* `.eater-cards` is the vendored stylesheet's host and therefore its
              containment: every rule in that file begins with this class, so it
              reaches nothing above this element and nothing above it reaches in. */}
          <div class="eater-map__cards eater-cards">
            {CARDS.map((card) => (
              <div class="eater-map__card" data-eater-map-card={card.name}>
                {/* THE FLAT FACE, AND IT IS FORCED RATHER THAN TIDY (#190).
                    The Card is `preserve-3d`, so its blurred copy of the map and
                    its own content would both be at z: 0 under it, `z-index` would
                    stop deciding between them and the two would interleave — in
                    the prototype that made the details Card read as two smeared
                    panels. A `flat` face holds both, and the Card's edge slices
                    are that face's SIBLINGS, because a depth inside a flat face is
                    nothing at all. glass.ts.

                    THIS IS HALF A RULE ON ITS OWN, and it shipped as half for
                    three tickets (#203): the slices are siblings of the face so
                    that they can have a depth, and until the CARD was made a 3D
                    context there was no depth for them to have. Both halves are in
                    the stylesheet, next to each other, with the measurement.

                    It is in the template rather than built by the script so that
                    the box structure is the same for every reader, and it does not
                    change the Card's own size: the vendored root inside it carries
                    its frozen width and height, and this box is the only thing in
                    flow either way. */}
                <div class="eater-map__face">
                  {/* `display: contents`, so the wrapper this Card's own markup has
                      to arrive in — set:html replaces an element's children, and
                      the backdrop beside it would be one — generates no box at all
                      and the Card is laid out exactly as it was without it. */}
                  <div class="eater-map__surface" set:html={card.html} />
                  {/* AND THE SEARCH CARD'S SECOND VENDORED ROOT (#194): the app's
                      own results dropdown, hanging off the bar it is typed into.
                      A BOX and not `display: contents`, unlike the one above,
                      because the gap between the two has to be a length and the
                      collector's own normalisation writes `margin: 0 !important`
                      on every vendored root — so the margin goes on a box of this
                      Section's own, which is the one thing that rule cannot reach.

                      The row cap is restated HERE and inline, which is the one
                      value the collector cannot carry: the app sets
                      `--mobile-search-visible-results` inline on this shell, and a
                      root's inline style is exactly what the export strips, since
                      that is where the app writes a surface's placement. Without
                      it the panel falls back to the FOUR in its own `var()`
                      default and draws four rows inside a host box sized for two.
                      The number is the capture's, out of `cards.json` — the app's
                      variable, never a height. */}
                  {card.hung && (
                    <div
                      class="eater-map__hang"
                      style={`--mobile-search-visible-results: ${card.rows}`}
                      set:html={card.hung}
                    />
                  )}
                </div>
                <span class="eater-map__anchor" data-eater-map-anchor={card.name} />
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
          {/* THE FOURTH ANCHOR IS ON THE PLANE ITSELF, because the fourth point
              names the Slab: the offline basemap is the artefact the reader is
              already looking at rather than something standing on it. It is a
              sibling of the picture and inside the same camera, so it is turned
              by the same one rotation everything else on the plane is. */}
          <span class="eater-map__anchor" data-eater-map-anchor={SLAB_PART} />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    {/* An <ol> because each point is numbered on the page, and each names one
        part of the Exploded View — `point.part`, which the schema holds to
        exactly one point per part and exactly one part per point (#178).

        AFTER THE STAGE IN THE MARKUP AND NOT BEFORE IT, which is #179's doing and
        costs the band nothing: both boxes are placed by an explicit `grid-area`
        up there, so the drawing is left of the points whichever order they are
        written in. What the order buys is the COLLAPSE, where the column is
        source order and the four features have to read as a list BENEATH the
        Slab. Done with `order` or a `grid-row` instead, the reader looking at the
        page and the reader hearing it would be given two different sequences;
        moved here, there is one sequence in both regimes and in the document.

        `data-eater-map-watch` is the leader lines' redraw: this column changes
        height when a face arrives or a title wraps, and the Section's own box
        does not move when it does.

        THE HOOK IS WHERE A RULE LEAVES THIS ROW, and it is a BOX rather than a
        coordinate: its width is --eater-map-leader-reach, so the shoulder's two
        ends are its two edges and the script reads them off one rect instead of
        parsing a length out of a custom property, which computes to its own
        token stream rather than to pixels. leaders.ts. */}
    <ol class="eater-map__points" data-eater-map-watch>
      {content.points.map((point, at) => (
        <li>
          {/* THE NUMBER IS THE ORDINAL AND NOT A WORD, so it is derived from the
              list's own order rather than authored beside each point: an <ol>
              whose numbers are typed is two orders to keep in step, and the
              Editor would be offering a figure that means "which one this is".
              Drawn rather than left to a marker because a `list-style` number is
              not a thing a composition can set in the accent at its own size.

              `aria-hidden` on both marks in this row. The list is an <ol>, so a
              reader listening is already told which of four this is, and the icon
              says what the title beside it says. */}
          <div class="eater-map__mark" aria-hidden="true">
            <span class="eater-map__number">{`0${at + 1}.`}</span>
            <svg
              class="eater-map__icon"
              viewBox="0 0 24 24"
              focusable="false"
              stroke-linecap="round"
              stroke-linejoin="round"
              set:html={markFor(point.part)}
            />
          </div>
          <h3>{point.title}</h3>
          <p>{point.figure}</p>
          <span class="eater-map__hook" data-eater-map-hook={point.part} aria-hidden="true" />
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
    {/* THE LEADER LINES. One rule per point, drawn by leaders.ts from the point's
        hook to a zero-sized anchor at the part's own corner — which is inside
        the camera, so what the rule is given is where that corner LANDED rather
        than where the Card's untransformed box is. #178.

        Last in the composition and positioned, so it paints over the drawing
        without a z-index. `aria-hidden`, because the rules carry nothing that is
        not already in the four points' own words: they are how a reader LOOKING
        at the page knows which claim belongs to which piece, and a reader
        listening is given the same correspondence by reading the points in
        order. Empty of text for the same reason. `pointer-events: none` in the
        stylesheet, so the overlay cannot take a selection off the words under it.

        The polylines carry no `points` until a script arrives, so a reader whose
        scripts never came gets the Exploded View with no rules over it — which
        is the one thing the Lift's inversion cannot buy, and costs nothing that
        is not also in the text. NOTES.md. */}
    <svg class="eater-map__leaders" data-eater-map-leaders aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
      {content.points.map((point) => (
        <Fragment>
          <polyline data-eater-map-leader={point.part} />
          {/* THE SHOULDER'S DOT AND THE PART'S. Two marks per rule, drawn by the
              same module that draws the rule and for the same reason: where a
              projected corner landed is a fact only the compositor has. Both
              carry no `cx` until a script arrives, and an SVG circle with no
              centre and no radius paints nothing — so a reader whose scripts
              never came gets the Exploded View with no rules and no dots over
              it, which is the state the markup already reads as. */}
          <circle data-eater-map-knee={point.part} />
          <circle data-eater-map-tip={point.part} />
        </Fragment>
      ))}
    </svg>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* Scoped by the build. Astro rewrites every selector below with this
     component's own attribute, so none of them can match an element in another
     Section even by accident.

     THE SECTION PAINTS NO GROUND, and that is the rule rather than an omission.
     The page's ground is `html { background: var(--ground) }` in the Kernel and
     --ground is itself a crossing on --turn, so a Section that paints its own
     composes two crossings and puts a step across the page at its own top edge —
     which is what "banding" has meant both times it was reported. This Section
     inherits the page's ground and its ink in both themes, at the far end of a
     Turn that finished before the reader got here. */
  .eater-map {
    /* THE LIFT'S PLAYHEAD, AND IT IS NOT A TOKEN. 0 is the flat screenshot and 1
       is the finished Exploded View; the tilt, the depths and the gaps below are
       all it times a Token. It is declared HERE rather than in tokens.css
       because it is not a number the author chooses — `timeline.ts` writes it,
       the Editor's Timeline scrub is the control for it, and a second control in
       the Tokens panel would be two handles on one thing.

       IT RESTS AT 1, which is the whole of the inversion #177 asks for: the
       markup reads as the finished composition, the Timeline animates AWAY from
       it, and a reader whose scripts never arrived keeps the Exploded View
       rather than a half-built one. */
    --eater-map-lift: 1;

    /* IS THE EXPLODED VIEW DRAWN HERE? 0 where it is, 1 where the composition has
       collapsed to a flat full-bleed Slab with the four features under it (#179).

       IT IS NOT A TOKEN AND IT IS NOT A SECOND COPY OF THE BREAKPOINT — it is the
       breakpoint's ANSWER, declared where the breakpoint is. `timeline.ts` has to
       know whether there is anything to lift, and the two ways of telling it are
       a `matchMedia('(min-width: 1100px)')` in the module, which is the number
       written twice and drifting the day one of them moves, or this, which is the
       stylesheet saying what it already decided. A Token could not carry it at
       all: `check-source.mjs` refuses an `@media` block in tokens.css, and rightly
       — the Editor writes a value, not a breakpoint (src/sections/NOTES.md). */
    --eater-map-collapsed: 0;
    /* WHETHER THE SLAB IS A SOLID, which is the same kind of declaration as the
       two above it and is read by the stage rather than by this stylesheet. 1 in
       the band, 0 in the collapse — see there. */
    --eater-map-solid: 1;

    /* ---- THE MASTHEAD'S SIZE, WHICH IS ONE NUMBER WITH TWO ANSWERS ----------
       Inside the band it is the LANDING's, so PROJECTS occupies the Projects
       Panel masthead's own box to the pixel and the word does not move when the
       reader turns onto this screen (#191); out of the band there is no landing
       to read and no page turn to stand still across, so it is the Section's own
       clamp. Declared here as one name rather than as two `font-size` rules,
       because three other lengths on this composition are shares of it and would
       each need the same pair.

       NOT A TOKEN, and that is the point of the split: a size the author could
       drag is exactly the number that takes the word out of the box it is meant
       to be occupying. The three the author still has — the leading, the weight
       and the tracking — are in tokens.css, set to the Panel's own values, and
       the `eater-map` Check compares the two BOXES rather than any of them.

       THE TWO LITERALS BELOW ARE FACE METRICS AND NOT CHOICES, which is why they
       are not Tokens either: Host Grotesk's cap is 0.700 of its em and its cap
       top sits 0.080 below its line box's top edge at this leading. The Kernel
       writes the same two for the landing and the Panel writes them for itself;
       these are the third copy, and they exist so the title's FALLBACK — the one
       a reader with no script gets — can be stated at all. `title.ts` measures
       the real ink and overrides both. */
    --eater-map-mast: var(--eater-map-masthead-size);
    --eater-map-mast-cap: calc(0.7 * var(--eater-map-mast));
    --eater-map-mast-top: calc(0.08 * var(--eater-map-mast));

    box-sizing: border-box;
    display: grid;
    /* ONE COLUMN, AND THE MARGIN IS A PADDING. It was two, and the first was the
       Rail's — the Rail is the Kernel's now (#192), so the Section's own box
       starts at the page's margin rather than one grid column into it. Same left
       edge, one fewer box: --eater-map-side is what the Rail's column was, and it
       is still the page's own margin. */
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    /* STRETCH AND NOT CENTRE, and it is the difference between a composition
       that fits the screen and one that is taller than it. The stage is the one
       row's whole height and the four Points spread themselves down theirs, so
       both need the Section's own height handed to them. Centring would size the
       box to its content instead, and the content would then be whatever the four
       points and a picture-shaped box happen to add up to. */
    align-items: stretch;
    min-height: var(--fold);
    padding-block: var(--eater-map-inset);
    padding-inline: var(--eater-map-side);
    /* Clip, not hidden, and only across: `hidden` would make the Section a
       scroll container, which is what the Slab's full-bleed collapse would then
       scroll inside of. */
    overflow-x: clip;
    font-family: var(--face-panel);
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: var(--eater-map-leading);
  }

  /* ---- the composition: twelve columns and ONE row ------------------------
     THREE STANDING BLOCKS AND NOT TWO STACKED ONES (#191). The writing takes the
     left four columns, the drawing the middle six, and the four numbered Points
     the right two — each of them the whole height of the Section, and the two
     things in the left column pinned to its two ends: PROJECTS and the title at
     the head, the copy at the foot. That is the reference's own arrangement and it
     replaces the two-row grid the Section shipped with, where the writing was a
     band across the top and the picture everything under it.

     WHY THE MIDDLE SIX ARE WHERE THEY ARE. The drawing is centred in its column
     and its projected outline is WIDER than the column — a Slab as tall as the row
     projects to about 770px across at 1600x900 against a 731px column — so what
     the six columns actually buy is the CENTRE of the free space between the
     writing and the Points, which is where the outline hangs symmetrically. Give
     the stage a column count that is not centred between the other two and the
     drawing leans into one of them. */
  .eater-map__inner {
    /* The leader lines' overlay is absolute against THIS box, which is what
       makes every coordinate in leaders.ts independent of the scroll — a rule
       computed against the viewport would be dragged across the composition by
       the page turn. `relative` with no z-index is not a stacking context, so
       the Exploded View still sorts its own three dimensions. */
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(12, minmax(0, 1fr));
    /* ONE ROW, AND `minmax(0, 1fr)` RATHER THAN `1fr`: a bare `1fr` floors at the
       row's min-content, so the four points and the box would push the Section
       past the fold rather than being given what the screen has. NOTES.md. */
    grid-template-rows: minmax(0, 1fr);
    column-gap: var(--eater-map-gutter);
  }

  /* THE TWO ENDS OF THE LEFT COLUMN, and they are one grid area used twice
     rather than two rows. The head is start-aligned and the copy end-aligned in
     the SAME cell, which is what "the copy at the foot of the left column" is —
     the drawing beside it is the whole height of the Section, so a row boundary
     between the two would be a line across the composition that nothing else
     answers to. */
  .eater-map__head {
    grid-area: 1 / 1 / 2 / 5;
    align-self: start;
  }

  /* ---- how the capitalised things are set --------------------------------
     Listed rather than hoisted onto the Section: `case` on the copy would raise
     the hyphen in "offline-first" off its own baseline, and the figures are
     sentences rather than labels. */
  .eater-map__masthead,
  .eater-map__title,
  /* Each row is the containing block for its own hook, so `right: 100%` is this
     column's left edge rather than the page's. */
  .eater-map__points li {
    position: relative;
  }

  /* WHERE A RULE LEAVES ITS POINT, and how far it runs before it turns. A box
     rather than two numbers: its width is the reach Token spent on a real
     property, so leaders.ts reads the shoulder's two ends off one rect instead
     of parsing `0.9rem` out of a custom property, which is all a custom
     property's computed value ever is.

     ON THE ROW'S LEFT EDGE AND AT ITS TOP, which is the whole of what moving the
     Points across the page cost the leader lines (#191). `leaders.ts` decides
     which EDGE of this box a rule leaves from by which side of it the part is on,
     so the shoulder turned round on its own; which edge of the ROW the box stands
     on is a composition decision and this is it. And `top: 0` rather than half a
     line down, because the row's own rule is drawn across its top edge now — so
     the leader continues that line out of the Point rather than leaving from the
     middle of it. Derived and not a Token: a coordinate in the composition rather
     than a number the author chooses (ADR 0004). */
  .eater-map__hook {
    position: absolute;
    right: 100%;
    top: 0;
    width: var(--eater-map-leader-reach);
    height: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .eater-map__points h3 {
    text-transform: uppercase;
    font-feature-settings: 'case' 1;
  }

  /* A PLAIN MASTHEAD, AND IT PAINTS — and since #191 the word it paints is
     PROJECTS, in the Gallery's own box. The Projects Panel's own masthead is
     hidden because the Front Screen's Cut Title is already that Section's head;
     nothing is cut into this one's slot, because #172 measured what a second
     landing solve would cost and the answer was the Panel's whole marble corner.
     So this element is a second DRAWING of the word rather than a second Cut
     Title, standing where the first one stands at rest — and #193 is what makes
     the two one element. NOTES.md.

     `text-transform` rather than an authored capital, which is the Panel's rule
     too: the word is `Projects` in the Content and the composition is what shouts
     it. `case` so the tracking is the caps' own. */
  .eater-map__masthead {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--eater-map-mast);
    font-weight: var(--eater-map-masthead-weight);
    line-height: var(--eater-map-masthead-leading);
    letter-spacing: var(--eater-map-masthead-tracking);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    font-feature-settings: 'case' 1;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  /* THE SERIF PROJECT TITLE, AND BOTH ITS LENGTHS ARE FALLBACKS.
     `title.ts` writes `--eater-map-title-size` and `--eater-map-title-lead` off
     the two faces' real ink, which is the only way to hold a ratio between CAP
     HEIGHTS — the two Tokens the fallbacks below spend are ratios, and a
     grotesque's cap is about 0.70 of its em against this serif's 0.67.

     WHAT THE FALLBACKS ARE, so the four per cent is a known quantity rather than
     a surprise. The size applies the cap ratio to the masthead's SIZE instead of
     to its cap, which draws the title about 4% short. The lead puts the first
     LINE BOX where the first CAP TOP belongs — the masthead's baseline stands
     `leading − top − cap` above its own box bottom, and that difference is what
     the drop is measured from — so it ignores the title's own half-leading, which
     is a few pixels at this size. Both are a whole composition rather than a hole,
     which is the thing a reader with no script is owed.

     `--face-year` is the Kernel's role for Source Serif 4: the most upright and
     least written of the page's three serifs, and the one whose caps sit squarest
     under a grotesque masthead — which is what the `sub-source-serif` Variant
     argued for and what #191 adopted. */
  .eater-map__title {
    margin: var(
        --eater-map-title-lead,
        calc(
          var(--eater-map-title-drop) * var(--eater-map-mast-cap) -
            (
              var(--eater-map-masthead-leading) * var(--eater-map-mast) -
                var(--eater-map-mast-top) - var(--eater-map-mast-cap)
            )
        )
      )
      0 0;
    font-family: var(--face-year);
    font-size: var(
      --eater-map-title-size,
      calc(var(--eater-map-title-cap) * var(--eater-map-mast))
    );
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: var(--eater-map-title-leading);
    letter-spacing: var(--eater-map-title-tracking);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    font-feature-settings: 'case' 1;
    /* Near-white rather than the ink, so the masthead above it is still the
       loudest thing on the screen and the two read as a title and its project. */
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--ink), transparent var(--eater-map-title-veil));
  }

  /* `block` on the spans rather than a <br>, so the break is a property of the
     composition and not a character a screen reader has to read around. And
     `nowrap`, because the four lines are AUTHORED: a line re-broken by the column
     is a fifth line the composition never asked for, and the block is set to fit
     the column at the size the ratios give it. */
  .eater-map__title span {
    display: block;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* AT THE FOOT OF THE LEFT COLUMN (#191), which is the same grid area the head
     is in, end-aligned instead of start-aligned. It used to be a band across the
     top right, opposite the masthead; the reference reads down the left edge — the
     title at the top, the description at the bottom — and the drawing has the
     middle of the screen.

     THE RULE GOES WITH IT. A border on the left edge divided the copy from the
     masthead beside it; there is nothing beside it now, and the seam worth drawing
     is the one between the writing and the air above it. */
  .eater-map__copy {
    grid-area: 1 / 1 / 2 / 5;
    align-self: end;
    border-top: 1px solid
      color-mix(in oklab, var(--ink), transparent var(--eater-map-rule-veil));
    padding-top: var(--eater-map-close);
    font-size: var(--eater-map-copy-size);
    line-height: var(--eater-map-copy-leading);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .eater-map__copy p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .eater-map__copy p + p {
    margin-top: 1em;
  }

  /* THE FOUR POINTS, DOWN THE RIGHT EDGE (#191). They stood on the left, under
     the masthead they belonged to; the reference puts them beside the drawing they
     annotate, and each one is where its own leader line starts. Two columns rather
     than three, and that is not a taste: the drawing between them and the writing
     is centred in what those two leave, so a wider index moves the picture as well
     as itself.

     They take the whole of their column and spread themselves down it, the way the
     Panel's do: the air is what a point growing a paragraph spends, down to the
     step and no further. */
  .eater-map__points {
    grid-area: 1 / 11 / 2 / 13;
    align-self: stretch;
    margin: 0;
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-auto-rows: min-content;
    align-content: space-between;
    row-gap: var(--eater-map-step);
  }

  /* THE RULE ABOVE EACH POINT, and it is the leader line's own first inch. The
     accent at 36%, across the row and no further — the rule continues out of the
     row's left edge as the leader's shoulder, in the leader's own colour, so what
     the reader follows from a number to a part is one line that changes weight
     once. Drawn as a border on the row rather than as a box, so a point growing a
     second line of title does not move it. */
  .eater-map__points li {
    border-top: 1px solid
      color-mix(in oklab, var(--eater-map-accent), transparent var(--eater-map-accent-veil));
    padding-top: var(--eater-map-point-air);
  }

  /* The number and the icon on one line, the number on the baseline and the icon
     against the column's far edge: the pair reads as an annotation's label rather
     than as two things that happen to be near each other. */
  .eater-map__mark {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-end;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--eater-map-gutter);
  }

  .eater-map__number {
    font-size: var(--eater-map-number-size);
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: var(--eater-map-number-leading);
    color: var(--eater-map-accent);
  }

  /* `stroke` and no `fill`, at a weight that does not scale with the box: the
     marks are line drawings at 24 units, so a stroke stated in user units gets
     lighter as the icon grows and this one is stated to stay put. */
  .eater-map__icon {
    display: block;
    flex: none;
    width: var(--eater-map-icon-size);
    height: var(--eater-map-icon-size);
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--eater-map-accent);
    stroke-width: var(--eater-map-icon-weight);
  }

  .eater-map__points h3 {
    margin: var(--eater-map-point-air) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--eater-map-point-size);
    font-weight: var(--eater-map-point-weight);
    line-height: var(--eater-map-point-leading);
    letter-spacing: var(--eater-map-point-tracking);
    color: var(--ink);
    /* A title set to fit its column on one line at the window it was drawn at
       wraps on a narrower one rather than being made to fit by a size nobody
       chose; `balance` makes the two lines halves of a title. */
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .eater-map__points p {
    margin: var(--eater-map-figure-air) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--eater-map-figure-size);
    line-height: var(--eater-map-figure-leading);
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--ink), transparent var(--eater-map-muted-veil));
  }

  /* ---- the Exploded View --------------------------------------------------
     A picture of the map, and the app's own three surfaces on it. What makes it
     read as one screenshot rather than as three stickers on a photograph is that
     the whole plane is drawn in ONE coordinate system — the phone's — and
     everything on it is a share of that. What keeps that true once it comes
     apart is that there is ONE camera and ONE rotation, both on the plane, and
     every part of the drawing is inside it. NOTES.md. */
  /* `container-type: inline-size` HERE AS WELL AS ON THE SLAB, and it is what the
     cap below is measured against. An element is not its own container, so a
     `cqw` written on `.eater-map__slab` resolves against the nearest container
     ABOVE it — which is this box. That is the Frame's trap used deliberately: it
     is the only way for the Slab's own rule to say "a share of the stage's width"
     while the Slab is a container for everything on the plane.

     `inline-size` and NOT `size`, which would contain the height too: below the
     band this box's height is its contents' and `contain: size` would collapse it
     to nothing. */
  .eater-map__stage {
    grid-area: 1 / 5 / 2 / 11;
    align-self: stretch;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    container-type: inline-size;
  }

  /* THE PLANE. Its height is the stage's, times the one Token that says how much
     of that to take, capped by what the stage's WIDTH will carry once the outline
     is projected; its width follows from the capture's own proportion — so the
     phone is as big as the screen allows at every window in the band and the
     composition never has to be told a size. The cap is the rule below and #191's:
     the drawing stands in a column between the writing and the four Points now,
     so there are two ways to run out of room and the height alone is not one.

     `container-type: inline-size` is what lets everything on the plane be a
     share of the plane. It is the Frame's arrangement in the Projects Panel and
     it carries the Frame's trap with it: AN ELEMENT IS NOT ITS OWN CONTAINER, so
     a `cqw` written on this rule resolves against the STAGE — which the cap below
     uses on purpose, and which every other one has to avoid.
     Every one of them below is on a descendant.

     NOT CLIPPED, AND IT USED TO BE. While the Cards lay flat on the picture a
     Card hanging off its edge was a Card not lying on it, and `overflow: hidden`
     said so. Raised, a Card standing off the surface is exactly what the drawing
     is of — and the clip could not have stayed in any case: `overflow` other
     than `visible` makes an element a GROUPING element, and a grouping element's
     `transform-style: preserve-3d` is forced back to `flat`, so the clip alone
     would have flattened the Cards into the picture with no error and no
     warning. NOTES.md. */
  .eater-map__slab {
    position: relative;
    height: calc(var(--eater-map-slab-fill) * 100%);
    /* AND CAPPED BY WHAT THE STAGE IS WIDE ENOUGH TO CARRY, which is a different
       question from how tall it is and was not asked before #191. The drawing used
       to be a row's remainder with the four points beside the writing above it;
       the Points stand down the right edge now and the writing down the left, so
       the stage is a COLUMN between two things that have to stay legible — and a
       Slab sized only by the height runs its projected corners under both of them
       at the band's short corner. Measured: 599px of outline in 528px of room at
       1100x700, which is the rail overlay's paragraph with a phone drawn over it.

       THE PROJECTED WIDTH IS THE THING TO CAP, and it is not the Slab's width. A
       `w x h` rectangle turned by the swing spans `w·cos(s) + h·sin(s)` across the
       screen — the tilt takes nothing off it, because `rotateX` moves the y axis
       and leaves x alone — so with `h = tall·w` the outline is `w·(cos s + tall·sin
       s)` and the height that fits a stage `W` wide is `tall·W` over that. Both
       terms are the composition's own: the swing is the Token the plane is turned
       by, and `tall` is the capture's own proportion, written on the element beside
       the ratio it comes from. Drag either and this follows.

       ON `max-height` AND NOT ON `height`, so the aspect ratio survives it: the
       width is `auto` and derived from the ratio, so clamping the definite axis
       re-derives the other one. Clamping the width instead would hold the height
       and break the proportion.

       `100cqw` IS THE STAGE'S WIDTH AND NOT THIS BOX'S — an element is not its own
       container, and this box is one. See the stage's own rule. */
    max-height: calc(
      var(--eater-map-slab-fill) * 100cqw * var(--eater-map-slab-tall) /
        (
          cos(var(--eater-map-swing)) + var(--eater-map-slab-tall) *
            sin(var(--eater-map-swing))
        )
    );
    aspect-ratio: var(--eater-map-slab-ratio);
    /* A percentage translate resolves against the element's own box, so this is
       a share of the phone rather than of whatever the stage happens to be. */
    translate: calc(var(--eater-map-slab-shift) * 100%);
    container-type: inline-size;
  }

  /* THE ONE ROTATION EVERY PART SHARES, AND IT NEVER MOVES. The picture and all
     three Cards are inside this box, so there is exactly one projection on the
     page and no element carries a rotation of its own — which is what makes this
     one object taken apart rather than three stickers on a photograph.

     NOT A TERM OF THE LIFT, WHICH IS #189. Both angles used to be multiplied by
     `--eater-map-lift`, so the Slab tilted itself up as the reader arrived and
     progress 0 was the flat screenshot #176 built. It stands at its attitude the
     whole time now: the same solid, at the same size, with the same projected
     corners at both ends of the Lift and at both ends of the band, and only the
     Cards move. The Lift's flat end is the Cards down on a plane that is already
     turned, and the flat screenshot is no longer a state the drawing passes
     through.

     A PARALLEL PROJECTION, WHICH IS THE ABSENCE OF `perspective()` and not a
     camera pushed far away — a large camera only flattens towards this and never
     reaches it, which is why #180's isometric Variant had to restate this whole
     list. So there is no `perspective()`, no `--eater-map-camera` and no dolly:
     nothing converges, so nothing grows as it climbs and there is nothing for a
     camera pulling back to pay for. With no convergence, a depth reads as an
     offset along the plane's own normal — which is how an exploded view is drawn
     on paper.

     AND SINCE #207 THAT IS TAKEN LITERALLY: THERE IS NO THIRD DIMENSION HERE AT
     ALL. Every depth on this plane — each Card's rise off it, each slice of every
     solid's thickness — is written as the place that depth PROJECTS to, which
     under a parallel projection is exact rather than an approximation. So this
     element is `flat`, the whole Section composes into one 2D affine matrix, and
     the browser rasterises the Eater app's own text THROUGH that matrix instead
     of rasterising each Card square and resampling the texture onto a tilted
     plane. `--eater-map-depth-x` below is the projection; `edge.ts`'s `flatten`
     is the other half; NOTES.md has the before and after.

     WHICH RETIRES A TRAP RATHER THAN RENAMING IT. `preserve-3d` used to have to
     be HERE AND NOT ON THE SLAB, because the Slab is `container-type`, which is
     `contain: layout`, which is grouping, and a grouping element's `preserve-3d`
     is forced back to `flat` with no error and no warning. Nothing on this plane
     asks for a 3D rendering context now, so nothing can be silently denied one —
     but the rule is still true of any element that asks, and the two Variants
     that ask are in `variants.css`. NOTES.md carries it and the one under it. */
  .eater-map__plane {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    transform-style: flat;
    transform: rotateX(var(--eater-map-tilt)) rotateZ(var(--eater-map-swing));

    /* WHERE A UNIT OF DEPTH GOES, WHICH IN A PARALLEL PROJECTION IS A PLACE AND
       NOT A DIRECTION (#207). Under `rotateX(t) rotateZ(s)` and with no
       `perspective()`, a point a distance `d` BEHIND the plane projects to
       exactly the same screen point as one lying ON the plane, `d * tan(t)`
       further along it — `sin(s)` of that across and `cos(s)` of it down, in the
       plane's own axes. Solve `M * (a, b, 0) = M * (0, 0, -d)` for `a` and `b`
       and these two numbers are what comes out; there is no third term, because
       nothing converges and so nothing about the answer depends on where the
       point is.

       WHICH IS WHY THE DRAWING CAN BE FLAT AND STILL BE AN EXPLODED VIEW, and
       that is the whole of #207. A `preserve-3d` chain forces every descendant
       into its own render surface: the browser rasterises the Card SQUARE, in its
       own plane, and then the GPU resamples that texture onto the tilted one — so
       every glyph in the Eater app's markup arrives through a bilinear filter and
       the interface reads soft against the Points beside it, which is the whole
       complaint. Flattened, the accumulated transform is an ordinary 2D affine
       one, Skia draws the text THROUGH it, and the same words come out at the
       same angle with clean stems. Measured on the built page before and after;
       NOTES.md carries the comparison and the rects that show the drawing did not
       move by so much as a hundredth of a pixel.

       IT IS THE WHOLE CHAIN OR IT IS NOTHING, which cost a wrong fix. Flattening
       the Cards and leaving one `translateZ` on the Slab's slices leaves this
       plane a 3D scene, and a 3D scene composites ALL of its children — so the
       Cards go back into their own surfaces and the text is exactly as soft as it
       was. Measured: no change at all until the last depth on the plane was gone.
       `edge.ts`'s `flatten` is what takes the Slab's. */
    --eater-map-depth-x: calc(tan(var(--eater-map-tilt)) * sin(var(--eater-map-swing)));
    --eater-map-depth-y: calc(tan(var(--eater-map-tilt)) * cos(var(--eater-map-swing)));
  }

  /* The shipped attitude's own answer, for an engine without the trigonometric
     functions — the same bargain `--eater-map-app-scale` strikes below, and the
     same reason: an invalid `calc()` here is a Card with no rise and an edge with
     no thickness, which reads as a broken build rather than as a missing feature.
     52deg and 33deg give 0.6971 and 1.0734. */
  @supports not (scale: tan(atan2(1px, 1px))) {
    .eater-map__plane {
      --eater-map-depth-x: 0.6971;
      --eater-map-depth-y: 1.0734;
    }
  }

  .eater-map__still {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  /* THE ONE PIECE OF ARITHMETIC THE STAGE TURNS ON: the scale everything on the
     plane is drawn at is the Slab's drawn width over the phone Eater was driven
     at. Get it from anywhere else — a number chosen by eye, a share of the
     viewport — and the Cards are the right size at one window and floating over
     a map of the wrong scale at every other.

     `tan(atan2(a, b))` is `a / b`. CSS has no other way to divide one length by
     another: `calc()` requires the right-hand side of a `/` to be a number, and
     that is the spec rather than an engine's gap. NOTES.md.

     FLAT, AND THE CARDS' DEPTH IS SPENT ON THE PLANE INSTEAD (#207). This box was
     `preserve-3d` for three tickets, and it had to be while a Card's rise was a
     `translateZ`: without it the box flattens its children into itself and every
     Card's depth is discarded silently — the Cards still there, at the right
     scale, lying on the map for ever. What changed is not the rule but the rise.
     A depth in a parallel projection IS a place on the plane
     (`--eater-map-depth-x` on `.eater-map__plane`), so each Card is translated to
     that place and the third dimension is not asked for at all.

     AND THE POINT OF ASKING FOR LESS IS THE TEXT. `preserve-3d` is what puts each
     Card in its own render surface, rasterised square and then resampled onto the
     tilted plane by the GPU — which is what made the Eater app's own markup read
     soft against the Points beside it. Flat, the Card's transform composes into
     one 2D affine matrix that Skia draws the glyphs through. The drawing is the
     same drawing, to the pixel; only the raster is different. */
  .eater-map__cards {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    transform-style: flat;
    --eater-map-app-scale: tan(atan2(100cqw, var(--eater-map-app-w)));
  }

  /* An engine without the trigonometric functions gets a plausible screenshot
     rather than an interface drawn at the app's own pixel size over a map a
     third that scale, which is what an invalid scale would leave. The number is
     the ratio at the window the Checks run at. */
  @supports not (scale: tan(atan2(1px, 1px))) {
    .eater-map__cards {
      --eater-map-app-scale: 0.72;
    }
  }

  /* Each Card at its own corner of the picture, in the picture's own units, and
     at its own depth off it. The Cards carry their own frozen pixel sizes — they
     are the app's markup at the window it was exported at (NOTES.md) — so the
     scale is a transform and never a width: a width would reflow another
     repository's interface.

     THE CARD'S OWN PLAYHEAD, defaulting to the Section's. `timeline.ts` writes
     this one per Card so the three can climb one after another; nothing writes
     it when no script arrives, and the `var()` then hands each Card the Section's
     own 1 and the stack is up. Both are the same number and neither is a Token.

     TRANSLATE FIRST AND SCALE SECOND, and the order is the arithmetic: a
     transform list applies right to left, so the scale is the Card's own and the
     translate is in the PLANE's units — a depth in the picture's own coordinate
     system rather than in the app's. Written the other way round the app's own
     scale would multiply the depth and each Card would rise by a different
     amount for the same Token. All three distances are `cqw`, so they are shares
     of the Slab's WIDTH as everything else on the plane is — including the `top`
     below, which is why it is not a percentage (#189): a percentage `top`
     resolves against the containing block's HEIGHT, which put the placement and
     the slide in two different units while they read as a pair.

     AND THE RISE IS THE THIRD TERM OF THAT SAME TRANSLATE, not a `translateZ`
     (#207). In a parallel projection a Card `r` off the plane lands exactly where
     one lying ON the plane `r * tan(tilt)` further UP it would — so it is drawn
     there, and the whole Section stays a 2D affine drawing that the browser can
     rasterise text through. Read `--eater-map-depth-x` on `.eater-map__plane` for
     the derivation and for what the third dimension was costing. The MINUS is the
     direction: `--eater-map-depth-*` says where a unit of depth BEHIND the plane
     goes, and a Card rises in front of it.

     GLASS CANNOT BE CARRIED OFF A SURFACE, and this is the one thing the Lift
     costs that no Token can put back. A `backdrop-filter` samples what is painted
     behind an element IN ITS OWN PLANE, and there is no such thing once the plane
     is turned under a camera: Chromium hands the filter an empty backdrop and it
     becomes a no-op, silently, at the FIRST DEGREE of tilt. Measured — with the
     depths and the gap set to 0 and only the tilt standing, the app's frosted
     detail panel is already a sheet of clear glass with a sharp map behind its
     text, which is a composition a reader cannot read. NOTES.md.

     WHAT THIS USED TO DO ABOUT IT WAS THE OPPOSITE OF THE REFERENCE (#190). The
     composition mixed each of the app's three glass colours towards
     `--eater-map-plate` in step with the Card's climb, so a raised Card went
     opaque WHITE — legible, and the reverse of glass. The plate is gone and so is
     the mix; `glass.ts` gives each glass SURFACE a blurred copy of the Slab
     instead, offset by that surface's own place on it, and the palette below is
     what makes the Card dark enough to lie over a dark map. */
  .eater-map__card {
    --eater-map-card-lift: var(--eater-map-lift);
    --eater-map-card-rise: calc(
      var(--eater-map-card-lift) * var(--eater-map-rise) * var(--eater-map-card-depth)
    );
    /* THE WHOLE OF THE SCALE THIS CARD IS DRAWN AT, as one name, because two
       things need it and one of them is not in this file. The `scale()` below is
       the obvious reader; the other is `edge.ts`, which draws the Card's edge
       slices as offsets in the CARD's own units and has to divide the plane's
       depth by exactly this to get there. Written twice it would be one number
       with two spellings, and the failure — an edge at the right thickness on one
       window and the wrong one on the next — is silent. */
    --eater-map-card-total: calc(
      var(--eater-map-app-scale) * var(--eater-map-card-scale)
    );
    /* WHERE THE CARD SLIDES TO as it climbs, along the plane, in both of the
       plane's own directions and as a share of the Slab's width. Two Tokens per
       Card rather than one gap spent by the depth (#189): the three depths are
       within 12% of each other now, so `(0.5 - depth)` would slide all three the
       same way by the same amount, which is a translation of the drawing rather
       than a gap in it.

       BOTH FALL BACK TO 0, and that is not belt and braces. A `var()` with no
       fallback makes the whole `transform` below invalid the moment a Card has no
       per-name block — so a fourth Card added to cards.ts without one would be
       drawn UNROTATED at the app's own frozen pixel size over a map at 0.72 of
       it, which reads as a broken vendoring rather than as a missing Token. 0 is
       a Card that does not slide, which is the honest reading. */
    --eater-map-card-drift-x: calc(
      var(--eater-map-card-lift) * var(--eater-map-card-slide-x, 0)
    );
    --eater-map-card-drift-y: calc(
      var(--eater-map-card-lift) * var(--eater-map-card-slide-y, 0)
    );
    position: absolute;
    left: calc(var(--eater-map-card-x) * 100cqw);
    top: calc(var(--eater-map-card-y) * 100cqw);
    transform-origin: 0 0;
    transform: translate(
        calc(
          (
              var(--eater-map-card-drift-x) - var(--eater-map-card-rise) *
                var(--eater-map-depth-x)
            ) * 100cqw
        ),
        calc(
          (
              var(--eater-map-card-drift-y) - var(--eater-map-card-rise) *
                var(--eater-map-depth-y)
            ) * 100cqw
        )
      )
      scale(var(--eater-map-card-total));
  }

  /* ---- THE CARDS ARE DARK, AND IT IS AN OVERRIDE RATHER THAN A FORK ---------
     A light interface floating over a dark map is what #187 complained about, and
     the vendored export already publishes every colour its surfaces are made of.
     So this block sets THOSE NAMES and nothing else: no rule in `cards.css` is
     edited, nothing here selects into another repository's markup, and a
     re-vendoring — which rewrites that file and not this one — leaves the dark
     theme standing.

     ON THE CARD AND NOT ON `.eater-map__cards`, which is the vendored host itself.
     A declaration on the host would be `.eater-cards` against `.eater-cards` — the
     same weight, settled by whichever stylesheet the bundler emitted second, and
     `cards.css` is an ordinary import while this block is Astro's own. One element
     in and it is not a contest at all: the values inherit down to every surface,
     which is exactly how the plate mix reached them before.

     THE THREE GLASS COLOURS ARE TOKENS AND THE REST ARE NOT, and the split is
     which of them the author has an opinion about. How dark and how transparent a
     Card's surface is IS the composition #190 is about — it is what "the map is
     visibly present through the Cards" comes down to — and the labels, separators
     and rims are that palette's consequences rather than choices.

     `--glass-sheet-float` IS THE DETAILS SHEET'S OWN BACKGROUND at the viewport
     the Cards were exported at, which is #190's "floating actions bar": the
     `.actions` row is `background: transparent` down there, so the only thing that
     could veil the paragraph under it is the sheet, and it drops to near-nothing.
     The paragraph then sits on the blurred map rather than on a fogged panel.

     THE APP'S OWN `backdrop-filter`s ARE LEFT ALONE, and that is deliberate. In
     the band they sample an empty backdrop and are no-ops, so overriding them
     would buy nothing; below the band the plane is flat and they are exactly the
     frost a screenshot has. */
  .eater-map__card {
    --label: #f3f3f5;
    --label-secondary: rgba(235, 235, 245, 0.66);
    --label-tertiary: rgba(235, 235, 245, 0.38);
    --separator: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
    --separator-strong: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.17);
    --fill-secondary: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
    --blue: #4c9dff;
    --blue-tint: rgba(76, 157, 255, 0.18);
    --green: #40d867;
    --glass: var(--eater-map-glass);
    --glass-sheet: var(--eater-map-glass-sheet);
    --glass-sheet-float: var(--eater-map-glass-float);
    /* The results dropdown's own material, and the fourth name in this list
       rather than a reuse of one above it: `cards.css` publishes three glasses
       and `results.css` a fourth, because the app draws the dropdown thicker
       than the pill it hangs from — it is the one surface with two rows of
       reading on it. #194. */
    --glass-thick: var(--eater-map-glass-thick);
    --glass-rim: inset 0 0.5px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.26),
      inset 0 0 0 0.5px rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.13);
    --glass-rim-strong: inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.3),
      inset 0 0 0 0.5px rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.16);
    /* The host paints `color: rgb(0, 0, 0)` on itself, so the ink has to be said
       as well as the palette — read back off the name above rather than repeated,
       which is a property reading a custom property and not a cycle. */
    color: var(--label);
    /* So the text field's caret and any UA-drawn part of a control are dark-theme
       too. The app's own dark mode is out of scope (#187) and this is not it: it
       is one declaration about how a picture of it is painted here. */
    color-scheme: dark;
  }

  /* THE FLAT FACE THE BACKDROP AND THE CONTENT BOTH LIVE IN. A `preserve-3d`
     parent puts every child in one 3D space, so a copy of the map at z: 0 and the
     Card's own markup at z: 0 are sorted by depth rather than by `z-index` and
     they interleave — the details Card read as two smeared panels in the
     prototype. Flat, the face composites its children into one plane and paint
     order decides, which is what a stack of glass and ink wants.

     `relative`, because the backdrops are absolutely positioned against it, and it
     is the Card's only in-flow child so the Card is still exactly the size of the
     vendored surface inside it. */
  .eater-map__face {
    position: relative;
    transform-style: flat;
  }

  /* AND THE CARD IS FLAT TOO, WHICH IS A DIFFERENT ANSWER TO THE SAME QUESTION
     #190 AND #203 EACH ANSWERED WITH `preserve-3d` (#207).

     The question is why a Card's edge slices have to be somewhere other than the
     flat face. #190 moved them OUT of it, on the grounds that "a depth inside a
     flat face is nothing at all" — true, but they landed in the CARD, which was
     `flat` too, so their depth went on being nothing. Measured: every one of the
     details Card's wall slices sat at `translateZ(-3.6px)` with a screen rect
     IDENTICAL to the face's, so all twenty-four painted underneath it and the
     Cards read as decals lying on the map. #203 fixed that by making the Card
     `preserve-3d`, which worked and cost the text its sharpness — a
     `preserve-3d` descendant is rasterised in its own plane and resampled onto
     this one, so every glyph of the vendored app came through a bilinear filter.

     WHAT IS HERE NOW KEEPS BOTH. There is no `translateZ` left under this element
     to be discarded: `edge.ts` draws each slice at the place its depth projects
     to (`--eater-map-depth-x` on `.eater-map__plane`), in this Card's own units,
     which is why `--eater-map-card-total` is declared above. So the edge stands
     off the face by exactly what it stood off by before, the drawing is the same
     drawing, and the Card is one flat affine layer that text can be drawn
     through.

     DRAGGING THE THICKNESS STILL MOVES IT, which is the thing #203 was really
     about: `--eater-map-card-thickness` is a term of the depth those offsets are
     computed from, so four times the depth is four times the offset. The
     `eater-map` Check measures the screen distance between a surface's
     shallowest and deepest wall slices and requires it off zero, and that
     assertion is unchanged by any of this — it was written against the RENDERED
     result rather than against the mechanism, so it still catches the failure it
     was written for.

     WHAT THE FLAT CARD GIVES BACK is `z-index`: a flat parent composites its
     children by paint order, so the slices are ordered by the DOM rather than
     sorted by depth. `edge.ts` puts them in the order a depth sort would have
     painted them — deepest first — which is why it inserts each slice before the
     last one rather than after it. */
  .eater-map__card {
    transform-style: flat;
  }

  /* WHERE A HUNG SURFACE STANDS, AND THE CLEARANCE IS DERIVED RATHER THAN TYPED
     (#194). The search results dropdown hangs off the bar it is typed into, and
     it has to clear that bar's EDGE and not its face — a wall of thickness `t`
     sinks into the plane, so it projects DOWN-SCREEN by `t x sin(tilt)`, while a
     gap `g` along the plane projects down by only `g x cos(tilt)`. The dropdown
     therefore clears only when

         g  >  t x tan(tilt)

     which is about five plane-px at today's thickness and tilt — and rendered at
     exactly that minimum the two are still a hairline apart, because a minimum
     behaves like one. So twice it. WRITTEN AGAINST THE TWO TOKENS AND NOT AS A
     THIRD NUMBER: a gap typed here would go on reading as correct while a later
     change to the thickness or the tilt walked the edge back into the bar above
     it.

     IN THE CARD'S OWN UNITS, which is why the thickness — a share of the SLAB's
     width — is spent against the phone's width and divided by the Card's boost.
     `glass.ts` writes the same two conversions for the same reason, and its
     `Solid.filletBack` says why a Card's face and its depth are different units.

     NOT MULTIPLIED BY `--eater-map-solid`. Below the band the plane is flat and
     no wall projects anywhere, so the derivation has nothing to say down there —
     but a dropdown touching the bar it hangs from is not an interface the app
     has, and what is left at this size is within a couple of pixels of the app's
     own gap. So it is a gap out there and a clearance in the band. */
  .eater-map__card {
    --eater-map-results-clear: calc(
      2 * var(--eater-map-card-thickness) * var(--eater-map-app-w) *
        tan(var(--eater-map-tilt)) / var(--eater-map-card-scale)
    );
  }

  /* An engine without the trigonometric functions gets the same clearance at the
     tilt the composition ships at, for the same reason the scale above it has a
     constant to fall back on: what would otherwise happen is an invalid `calc()`,
     a margin of zero, and a dropdown lying against the search bar's wall. Only
     `tan(tilt)` is pinned; the thickness is still read as the Token it is. */
  @supports not (scale: tan(atan2(1px, 1px))) {
    .eater-map__card {
      --eater-map-results-clear: calc(
        2 * var(--eater-map-card-thickness) * var(--eater-map-app-w) * 1.28 /
          var(--eater-map-card-scale)
      );
    }
  }

  /* THE BOX A HUNG SURFACE ARRIVES IN, and it is a box rather than
     `display: contents` for one mechanical reason: the vendored root inside it
     carries `margin: 0 !important` from the collector's own normalisation, so
     the clearance above it cannot be written on the vendored element at all.
     This is the element that rule does not reach.

     `glass.ts` reads this class as well, which is how a surface knows it is hung:
     the ruler is given a clearance of zero and a hung surface's measured top is
     handed back with the clearance added as an expression, so the number written
     down stays frozen to the export's viewport and the offset stays live. */
  .eater-map__hang {
    margin-top: var(--eater-map-results-clear);
  }

  /* The wrapper `set:html` needs and the layout does not: `contents` generates
     no box, so the Card is laid out exactly as it was when its own markup was
     the Card's only child. cards.css has no child combinator in it — checked —
     so nothing in the vendored stylesheet notices either way. */
  .eater-map__surface {
    display: contents;
  }

  /* WHERE A LEADER LINE ENDS, and the whole reason the anchors are elements
     rather than arithmetic. This box is ZERO-SIZED and lives INSIDE the camera,
     so it projects to a POINT and `getBoundingClientRect()` hands back that
     corner's actual position on screen. Reading the Card's own rect instead
     gives the axis-aligned bounding box of the projected quad, whose corners are
     nowhere on the Card once it is turned — which looks right at the flat end of
     the Lift and wrong at every other frame of it. leaders.ts.

     A share of the part it is on, so it is a corner at every window and at every
     size the Slab is drawn at, and so dragging it in the Editor moves it around
     the Card rather than around the page. */
  .eater-map__anchor {
    position: absolute;
    left: calc(var(--eater-map-anchor-x) * 100%);
    top: calc(var(--eater-map-anchor-y) * 100%);
    width: 0;
    height: 0;
  }

  .eater-map__anchor[data-eater-map-anchor='search'] {
    --eater-map-anchor-x: var(--eater-map-anchor-search-x);
    --eater-map-anchor-y: var(--eater-map-anchor-search-y);
  }

  .eater-map__anchor[data-eater-map-anchor='lines'] {
    --eater-map-anchor-x: var(--eater-map-anchor-lines-x);
    --eater-map-anchor-y: var(--eater-map-anchor-lines-y);
  }

  .eater-map__anchor[data-eater-map-anchor='details'] {
    --eater-map-anchor-x: var(--eater-map-anchor-details-x);
    --eater-map-anchor-y: var(--eater-map-anchor-details-y);
  }

  .eater-map__anchor[data-eater-map-anchor='slab'] {
    --eater-map-anchor-x: var(--eater-map-anchor-slab-x);
    --eater-map-anchor-y: var(--eater-map-anchor-slab-y);
  }

  /* ---- the leader lines --------------------------------------------------
     Four thin rules over the composition, joining each numbered point to the
     part of the Exploded View it names. The geometry is leaders.ts's — nothing
     here says where a rule goes, because where a projected corner LANDED is a
     fact only the compositor has.

     `overflow: visible`, because a Card raised off the Slab can stand outside
     the composition's own box and the rule to it has to follow. The Section's
     own `overflow-x: clip` is still what stops anything leaving the page.

     The rules are painted from --ink like every other quiet mark in this Section
     — mixed towards transparent rather than towards a colour, so they sit
     correctly in both themes at the far end of a Turn that finished before the
     reader arrived. */
  .eater-map__leaders {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    overflow: visible;
    pointer-events: none;
    fill: none;
    stroke: color-mix(in oklab, var(--ink), transparent var(--eater-map-leader-veil));
    stroke-width: var(--eater-map-leader-weight);
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
  }

  /* THE TWO DOTS, and they are filled where the rules are stroked — so `fill:
     none` on the overlay has to be undone per circle rather than left to be
     inherited into nothing. The one on the PART is lit: the ink straight, at the
     larger radius, which is what makes it read as the thing the number points at.
     The one at the shoulder is the rule's own colour and smaller, and says only
     that the line turned.

     `stroke: none` on both. A circle inherits the overlay's stroke, and a filled
     dot with a half-pixel ring round it in another colour is a dot drawn twice. */
  .eater-map__leaders circle {
    stroke: none;
  }

  /* AND EACH RADIUS IS GATED ON THE CENTRE THE SCRIPT SETS. An SVG circle with no
     `cx` sits at the overlay's own origin, so a radius written unconditionally
     here would paint four dots stacked in the composition's top-left corner for a
     reader whose scripts never arrived. `r`'s initial value is 0 and a circle of
     radius 0 renders nothing, so gating on `[cx]` is the same shape the polylines
     above already have — they carry no `points` until a script comes, and paint
     nothing until then.

     The radius is a Token spent on a real property rather than parsed out of a
     custom property by the script, which is the rule the shoulder's reach is
     under: a custom property's computed value is its own token stream, so
     `0.2rem` read from script is the string `0.2rem`. */
  .eater-map__leaders [data-eater-map-tip][cx] {
    r: var(--eater-map-leader-tip);
    fill: var(--ink);
  }

  .eater-map__leaders [data-eater-map-knee][cx] {
    r: var(--eater-map-leader-knee);
    fill: color-mix(in oklab, var(--ink), transparent var(--eater-map-leader-veil));
  }

  .eater-map__card[data-eater-map-card='search'] {
    --eater-map-card-x: var(--eater-map-card-search-x);
    --eater-map-card-y: var(--eater-map-card-search-y);
    --eater-map-card-depth: var(--eater-map-card-search-depth);
    --eater-map-card-slide-x: var(--eater-map-card-search-slide-x);
    --eater-map-card-slide-y: var(--eater-map-card-search-slide-y);
  }

  .eater-map__card[data-eater-map-card='lines'] {
    --eater-map-card-x: var(--eater-map-card-lines-x);
    --eater-map-card-y: var(--eater-map-card-lines-y);
    --eater-map-card-depth: var(--eater-map-card-lines-depth);
    --eater-map-card-slide-x: var(--eater-map-card-lines-slide-x);
    --eater-map-card-slide-y: var(--eater-map-card-lines-slide-y);
  }

  .eater-map__card[data-eater-map-card='details'] {
    --eater-map-card-x: var(--eater-map-card-details-x);
    --eater-map-card-y: var(--eater-map-card-details-y);
    --eater-map-card-depth: var(--eater-map-card-details-depth);
    --eater-map-card-slide-x: var(--eater-map-card-details-slide-x);
    --eater-map-card-slide-y: var(--eater-map-card-details-slide-y);
  }

  /* ---- one column, outside the band the composition is fitted to ---------
     The same move the Projects Panel makes at the same width: the two-column
     drawing stops earning its columns, so it becomes a stack as tall as it needs
     to be, with the Rail a row across the top instead of an index down an edge.

     AND THE EXPLODED VIEW COLLAPSES WITH IT (#179). An Exploded View is a drawing
     fitted to a wide window: it spends width on a camera, on the distance the
     pieces draw apart along the plane, and on a stage that is a row's remainder,
     and a column has none of those to give. So out here the Slab lies flat and
     runs to both edges of the window, and the four features are an ordinary list
     under it.

     ONE COMPOSITION AND NOT THREE. It is written as rules on the MARKUP'S OWN
     RESTING STATE rather than as anything a script does, which is what makes the
     narrow reader, the reader who asked for no motion and the reader whose
     scripts never arrived all get the same drawing — one thing to build, and one
     equality for the Check to assert rather than three separate compositions.
     `timeline.ts` reads --eater-map-collapsed and never runs the Lift down here,
     so the flat state is where the drawing STAYS rather than where it starts. */
  @media not all and (min-width: 1100px) {
    .eater-map {
      --eater-map-collapsed: 1;
      /* FLAT, AND SAID HERE AS WELL AS ON THE PLANE BELOW. `transform: none`
         answers for the geometry the plane carries; this answers for the Cards,
         which are placed by the playhead rather than by the plane.

         AND IT IS WHAT MAKES THE GLASS TRUE DOWN HERE (#190). Each surface's copy
         of the map is offset by that Card's RESTING place on the Slab, so at
         playhead 0 — no drift, no rise — every copy lines up exactly with the map
         beneath it and the collapsed drawing is an honestly frosted screenshot.
         A Card left at the raised end would be the same shape with a map behind
         its glass that is not the map under it, which is the difference a
         comparison of rects alone cannot see. */
      --eater-map-lift: 0;
      /* AND NO THICKNESS, for the same reason and by the same route. The Slab's
         depth stopped being a term of the playhead in #189, so `--eater-map-lift:
         0` no longer takes it away — and an edge is a thing an Exploded View has,
         not a thing a full-bleed picture at the top of a column has. The stage
         multiplies its depth and its radius by this, so the whole solid closes up
         to the flat picture down here and opens again on a resize back into the
         band without anything being re-mounted. Declared beside the playhead and
         not in tokens.css for the reason --eater-map-collapsed is: it is a regime
         and not a number the author chooses. */
      --eater-map-solid: 0;
      /* The columns and the side padding are the base rule's out here too, since
         the Rail left the grid (#192) — they are restated because this block used
         to be undoing a two-column grid and a right-only padding, and stating
         them costs nothing while a reader wondering whether the collapse still
         answers for them costs something. */
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      min-height: 0;
      padding-inline: var(--eater-map-side);
    }

    /* NO ROW GAP, AND THE SEAMS ARE MARGINS INSTEAD. A grid's row gap is one
       length for every row it has, and the seams down this column are different
       sizes of decision — the head to the copy is not the copy to the picture. */
    .eater-map__inner {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      grid-template-rows: none;
      row-gap: 0;
    }

    /* THE FOUR AUTHORED LINES MAY RE-BREAK OUT HERE, and that is the one thing
       the collapse takes off the title. In the band they are set to fit a column
       four twelfths wide at a size the two ratios give them; down here the column
       is the window, which is 390px on the phone this collapse exists for, and
       `nowrap` would run the longest of them off the screen. The break is still
       the composition's wherever the line fits. */
    .eater-map__title span {
      white-space: normal;
    }

    /* `grid-row: auto` AND NOT ONLY `grid-column: 1`, which is the trap this
       reset has: the four areas above are `grid-area` shorthands, so each pins a
       ROW as well as a column. Taking the columns back and leaving the rows
       stacks the head on the copy and the points inside the picture — two pairs
       of overlapping text, which is what it did. Auto-placed, they come out in
       source order, which is the order this column reads in. */
    .eater-map__head,
    .eater-map__copy,
    .eater-map__points,
    .eater-map__stage {
      grid-column: 1;
      grid-row: auto;
      align-self: auto;
    }

    /* The rule is already across the copy's top edge in both regimes since #191,
       so what the collapse still owes it is the seam above it: in the band the
       copy stands at the foot of a column and the air above it is whatever the
       column has left, and down here it is one block after another. */
    .eater-map__copy {
      margin-top: var(--eater-map-move);
    }

    .eater-map__points {
      margin-top: var(--eater-map-act);
      row-gap: var(--eater-map-move);
    }

    /* FULL-BLEED, AND THE NEGATIVE MARGIN IS WHY IT IS NOT `100vw`. The Section
       carries the page's margin on both edges out here, and a picture that runs
       to the window's edges has to spend both of them back. `100vw` would be the
       obvious way and is wrong by exactly the scrollbar's gutter on every
       platform that reserves one — the trap `projects-panel` paid for and
       scripts/checks/NOTES.md records, and it is worse here than it was there,
       because this box is CENTRED in what it overflows and would hang the error
       half out of each edge. A margin is stated against the box's own containing
       block, so it lands on the window whether or not there is a gutter. */
    .eater-map__stage {
      margin-top: var(--eater-map-act);
      margin-inline: calc(-1 * var(--eater-map-side));
    }

    /* NO LEADER LINES OUT HERE, and absent rather than redrawn (#178). The
       composition is one column with the four points BENEATH the picture, so a
       rule from a point to its part would run back up the page and join a
       paragraph to a corner off the top of the screen. They may simply go,
       because everything they carry is in the points' own words — and they have
       to, because a rule drawn before a resize would otherwise still be lying
       across the stack afterwards. leaders.ts asks this declaration rather than
       repeating the breakpoint, so the collapse is decided in one place. */
    .eater-map__leaders {
      display: none;
    }

    /* THE SLAB IS THE WHOLE OF THAT, and the proportion carries the height, which
       is the same way round as the band reads it — there the height is the row's
       remainder and the width follows, here the width is the window and the
       height follows. What went with it is `--eater-map-slab-cap`: a ceiling on
       the picture's width was the interim's, and full-bleed is a picture with no
       ceiling by definition (#179). And `translate` goes too — the shift slides
       the Slab across a stage it is narrower than, and out here it is the stage. */
    .eater-map__slab {
      height: auto;
      width: 100%;
      translate: none;
      /* AND NO CAP ON THE HEIGHT. The cap in the band is a bound on the drawing's
         PROJECTED outline, and nothing is projected down here — the plane carries
         no transform at all — so the same expression would be clamping a flat
         picture to the width its outline WOULD have had if it were turned, which
         is 0.5 of the picture. Full-bleed is a picture with no ceiling by
         definition (#179), and this is the second thing that had to say so. */
      max-height: none;
    }

    /* NO PART OF THE PROJECTION SURVIVES, AND THIS IS NOW THE ONLY THING SAYING
       SO. It used to be belt and braces — every angle was a term of
       --eater-map-lift, so the 0 above already flattened the plane and this said
       it outright for the things that read the drawing rather than look at it.
       Since #189 the attitude is a constant the playhead does not touch, so
       without these two declarations a column below the band would carry a Slab
       tilted 52 degrees. `transform-style: flat` goes with it for the reason it
       always did: the Cards would otherwise still stand in a 3D rendering
       context, and the leader lines, a Variant and a Check would each be asking
       a flattened projection rather than a plane. */
    .eater-map__plane {
      transform: none;
      transform-style: flat;
    }

    .eater-map__cards {
      transform-style: flat;
    }
  }

  /* ---- the landing band --------------------------------------------------
     THIS SECTION JOINS THE LANDING MEASURE HERE, AND #191 IS THE DECISION THAT
     PUT IT ON THAT LIST. It used to read two of the Kernel's lengths and no more
     — the page's top margin and its left one — and say in NOTES.md that reading
     them was deliberately not joining the measure, because this Section had no cut
     word and was a function of none of `--landing-w`, `--landing-cap` or
     `--landing-mast-top`. It has the Gallery's WORD now, in the Gallery's own box,
     so it is a function of all three. That is a change of decision rather than a
     drift, and #193 — one persistent PROJECTS for the page — is what it is on the
     way to.

     THE TOP MARGIN IS THE PANEL'S OWN LIFT, and this is the whole of "to the
     pixel". `--landing-top` is where the word's CAP TOP comes to rest and
     `--landing-mast-top` is how far the cap top sits below its line box's own top
     edge — so the difference is where the masthead's BOX goes, which is exactly
     what the Panel spends its `--projects-panel-lift` on. The Panel's port sits
     that far above its Section's top edge and its masthead's box sits on that
     edge; this Section's port IS its top edge, so the same distance is its top
     padding. Both mastheads then stand at the same place on the screen at rest,
     which is what the `eater-map` Check compares.

     AND THE LEFT MARGIN IS `--landing-side` EXACTLY, which is the second half of
     it. That margin is the Rail's column — the Kernel's Rail stands in it now
     (#192), pinned to the window — so the composition starts at its far edge,
     where the Panel's own inner starts and where PROJECTS stands on both
     screens. It was a grid column of that width with no gap after it, and it is
     the Section's own padding now; the word's left edge is unmoved either way,
     and anything between the two would push it right of the word it is meant not
     to have moved from.

     The foot keeps the page's own bottom margin: the drawing does not stand on
     the page's bottom edge the way the Panel's Plinth does. */
  @media (min-width: 1100px) and (min-height: 700px) {
    .eater-map {
      --eater-map-mast: var(--landing-mast-size);
      padding-block: calc(var(--landing-top) - var(--landing-mast-top))
        var(--landing-inset);
      padding-inline: var(--landing-side);
    }
  }
</style>
